\section{S3 Fig and the definition of a common variant}{


Both series of S3 Fig use the pooled definition of a common variant: minor
allele frequency at least 0.01 in the three populations taken together.  The
1000 Genomes series is computed from the public phase 3 genotypes by
\verb{scripts/empirical/1000g_frequencies.sh}, which writes the counts shipped in
\code{data/allele_frequencies/thousand_genomes_common_af.RDS}; the genotypes
themselves are not redistributed here.

The definition matters.  Under the pooled definition the African sample has
the highest average minor allele frequency, 0.1244 in the simulation and
0.1398 in 1000 Genomes.  Requiring a variant to be common in every population
separately selects against variation private to any one of them and reverses
that ordering, giving 0.2152 for the African sample against 0.2398 for the
European one.  The trait simulations draw from the pooled panel, so the
pooled definition is the one that matches them.

An earlier version of this figure drew a simulated series that no definition
shipped here reproduces; it ran at roughly 0.125, 0.089 and 0.086 for the
African, European and East Asian samples, which matches neither.  The series
drawn now is the pooled one.
}

\section{The published Figure 2 image predates the distributed results}{


\code{\link[=figure_02]{figure_02()}} reproduces the \emph{data} behind Figure 2, but not the \emph{image} in
the manuscript.  At a heritability of 0.1 the published labels read 0.996,
0.979 and 0.970 for the three pairs, whereas the distributed replicates give
1.292, 1.231 and 1.128.  At a heritability of 0.8 the two agree to three
decimal places, and at 0.4 they agree in one direction only.

The reason is that the published image was drawn from an earlier run and was
never redrawn after the replicates were regenerated.  The code and the data
here are consistent with each other; it is the manuscript image that is out
of date.
}

\section{Figure 1a is sensitive to the package versions}{


\verb{scripts/simulation/01_theory_validation.R} seeds every scenario
individually, so it does not depend on the number of cores, but rerunning it
does not reproduce the distributed
\code{data/theory_validation/distinct_populations.rds} exactly.  A rerun keeps 385
of 400 scenarios and gives a Pearson \code{r^2} of 0.99771, against 382 and
0.99747 in the distributed file.  None of the regenerated scenarios matches a
distributed one to within \code{1e-6}, so this is a different draw rather than a
rounding difference.

The reason is that no package versions are pinned, and the random streams of
\code{bnpsd} or of R itself have changed since the distributed file was produced.
The consequence is small but visible: panel a would annotate \code{r^2 = 0.998}
where the manuscript states 0.997.
}

\section{The coalescent genotypes cannot be regenerated exactly}{


\verb{scripts/coalescent/01_simulate_genotypes.py} accepts a \code{--seed}, but the run
that produced the genotypes behind Figures 2 to 4 and S1 to S8 was made
before that option existed and its seed was not recorded.  Any rerun
therefore produces a statistically equivalent but numerically different set
of genotypes, and every downstream result inherits that.  Results here are
reproducible in distribution, never bit for bit.
}

\section{The rare-variant pool is only approximately recovered}{


\verb{scripts/simulation/04_traits_rare_and_common.R} and
\verb{scripts/simulation/05_heritability_decomposition.R} define the rare pool as
the complement of the pooled common panel, which is the definition the
Methods give and which yields 2,817,430 variants, matching the fixation-index
analysis.  The surviving intermediate files from the published run instead
hold 907,919 rare variants, so a further filter was applied whose exact form
is not recorded.

Rerunning the reconstruction at the Figure 3 setting reproduces the published
asymmetry between African and non-African samples, and four of the six
ordered pairs agree within Monte Carlo error, with the remaining two about
2.3 standard errors away.  The qualitative result is therefore reproduced and
the precise values are not.
